# EVERY RULE BESIDE THE GRAIN IT DECLARES AND THE GRAIN ITS ROWS ACTUALLY HAVE. One row per rule.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THE CONTRACT'S COLUMNS NAME A SUBJECT MANY RULES DO NOT EXAMINE. `checks/all.sqlc`
#    returns `(rule, filing, layer, ...)` and says *which layer of which filing it is*. Wherever
#    the examined item is finer than a layer, a PART, a SLACK or a CLAIM, `(filing, layer)` is
#    not a key and the item's identity survives only inside the prose `detail`.
#    ⚠️ The tally is printed by `examples/soundness/main.rs` §4 rather than written here: it moved
#    when `claim` was added, which is the third subject and the largest of the three.
#
# ⭐⭐ WHICH SPLITS THE BAG INTO TWO THINGS THAT WERE BEING CALLED ONE. `invariance.sqlc` says
#    `checks/all.sqlc` IS A BAG because *several items per layer produce the same sentence*, and
#    that is true of TWO rules. For the other five every row says something DIFFERENT, and the
#    bag is a GRAIN artifact rather than a repetition one. ⭐ Its count-don't-subtract repair is
#    right for both, which is worth knowing: the repair was more general than its stated reason.
#
# ⛔⛔ THE LAW IS ONE-DIRECTIONAL AND THAT IS NOT A WEAKNESS. A rule declaring `layer` must have
#   exactly one row per `(filing, layer)`; a second row means it silently changed what it
#   examines and no other law here would see it, because the count moves and nothing says what
#   the count is OF. ⛔ The converse cannot be asserted: a part-grained rule may legitimately see
#   one part per layer in a corpus that happens to file one, and accusing it would be reading
#   luck as a declaration.
# ⭐⭐⭐ AND A KEY THAT IS UNIQUE IS NOT A KEY THAT RESOLVES, WHICH IS WHY THIS FILE NEEDED A
#    SECOND ARM. `unresolvable` counts the rows whose `(filing, layer)` names no filed layer.
#    ⛔ A NULL is not counted: a rule about a composition legitimately has no layer, and the
#    contract's column is nullable for exactly that. What is counted is a value that LOOKS like
#    a layer and is not, which is the state a reader and every join downstream cannot detect.
#
# ⚠️ ZERO IS A REPAIRABLE STATE AND NOT A PROPERTY OF THE CONTRACT. A rule whose subject is
#   finer than a layer reports the finer item's identity in `detail` and its own layer in the
#   column, because anything joining a verdict to a layer keeps only the pairs that resolve and
#   drops the rest in silence.
-- checks/roster.sqlc's declared subject against checks/all.sqlc's multiplicity AND its key.
SELECT r.slug,
       r.subject                                     AS declares,
       count(c.rule)                                 AS rows,
       count(DISTINCT (c.filing, c.layer))           AS layers,
       count(DISTINCT (c.filing, c.layer, c.detail)) AS sentences,
       r.subject = 'layer'
         AND count(c.rule) <> count(DISTINCT (c.filing, c.layer)) AS finer_than_declared,
       -- ⛔⛔⛔ THE ARM THE MULTIPLICITY ONE CANNOT BE. `finer_than_declared` asks whether the
       --    key is UNIQUE per row; this asks whether the key RESOLVES. A rule putting an
       --    address like `pm:nameplate/pm:amount claim 7` in the `layer` column satisfies the
       --    multiplicity arm by construction, because a value unique per row IS one row per key.
       --    ⭐ What makes the column wrong is exactly what makes that arm pass.
       count(c.rule) FILTER (WHERE c.layer IS NOT NULL AND l.layer IS NULL) AS unresolvable
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(checks/all.sqlc)
) c ON c.rule = r.rule
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(layers/every_layer.sqlc)
) l ON l.filing = c.filing AND l.layer = c.layer
GROUP BY r.slug, r.subject
